# upstream.allowlist — the complete, declared set of Chromium-owned files the
# Astro downstream integration is permitted to modify, and the shape each
# change is permitted to take.
#
# The companion of tools/overlay.allowlist, and deliberately the same shape, so
# a reader who knows one knows the other. That file governs what Astro COPIES
# into the Chromium tree; this one governs what Astro MODIFIES in files
# Chromium already owns.
#
# tools/check-upstream-delta.sh measures the Chromium checkout against the
# commit browser.lock.json pins, subtracts everything the declared legacy patch
# series and the declared pruning list account for, and treats the remainder as
# the //astro integration delta. It fails on:
#
#   * an integration delta in a Chromium-owned file with no entry here;
#   * ANY line removed from a Chromium-owned file by the integration delta;
#   * an entry whose integration additions exceed its declared max-added;
#   * added lines that do not have the shape the entry declares;
#   * an entry that no longer modifies anything (a stale declaration);
#   * a `planned` entry whose file has started to change;
#   * a measurement that covered nothing.
#
# Format:  <shape> <path> owner=<team> issue=<n> max-added=<n>
#
#   gn-append     A GN file that gains Astro-owned entries in existing lists.
#                 No new target, no new source, no conditional on an Astro
#                 value. Every added line must name the //astro module.
#
#   include-rule  A checkdeps DEPS file that gains "+astro" and nothing else.
#                 include_rules only; no other key may change.
#
#   call-hook     A .cc/.h that gains #include lines for astro/ headers and
#                 calls into the astro:: namespace. At least one added line
#                 must name the //astro module; the rest may only be the
#                 scaffolding that call requires — punctuation, a `return` of a
#                 literal, or a fragment of a function signature. No statement,
#                 no condition, no string literal, no call into anything but
#                 astro::. The rule is on the BLOCK rather than on every line
#                 because an override of an upstream virtual cannot satisfy a
#                 per-line rule: the signature upstream dictates names no Astro
#                 symbol and never can.
#
#   embedder-hook A file that gains a generic embedder extension point, whose
#                 hard-coded literal is replaced by an embedder-supplied value,
#                 or that declares or implements the override of such a point.
#                 This is the ONLY shape whose added lines are not required to
#                 name //astro, because the whole point of the shape is that
#                 the upstream code stays Astro-agnostic. It is therefore the
#                 weakest shape, bounded by max-added and by the removal rule
#                 alone — never grant it to a file that a call-hook would fit.
#
#   planned       A hook a later issue WILL need, declared here so its cap is
#                 reviewed once rather than invented under pressure. It grants
#                 NOTHING: a `planned` file that acquires an integration delta
#                 FAILS, and the fix is to promote the entry to a live shape.
#                 This is the difference between leaving room and granting
#                 permission in advance — 008-os-crypt-visibility.patch is the
#                 record of what granting in advance costs (findings §1).
#
# max-added bounds the added-line count. It is not a style rule: it is what
# makes "a small call" checkable rather than aspirational. Every cap below is
# derived from the DESIGNED shape in docs/astro-next/architecture/, with the
# arithmetic shown, and is deliberately NOT fitted to what the tree currently
# holds — a cap read off current reality ratifies drift instead of catching it.
# Raising one is a reviewed change to this file, which is the point.
#
# Whitespace-only added and removed lines are measured and reported separately
# and do not count against a cap or against the removal rule. A blank line can
# neither express a hook nor strip a feature, and patch application legitimately
# shifts them. The report prints the count so the exclusion is visible rather
# than silent.
#
# A LINE COMMENT — `#` in GN, `//` in C++ — is exempt from a shape's syntax
# check and from its module-reference requirement, for the same reason, but it
# still COUNTS against max-added: commentary in an upstream file is surface
# somebody maintains, and the cap is what bounds surface. Because comments are
# exempt from the per-line rule, every shape that requires a module reference
# also requires at least ONE added line to name //astro, so an entry whose
# whole delta is commentary cannot pass the shape check having hooked nothing.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REMOVING AN UPSTREAM LINE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# There is deliberately NO shape that permits removing an upstream line, and no
# attribute that can grant one. The historic reason is on record:
# 007-oxy-auth-build-hook.patch was 7 added lines against 36 REMOVED, and the
# removals stripped Safe Browsing notification content detection, accessibility,
# Screen AI, the preloading model, enterprise interstitials and the
# dangerous-download UI — under a patch named "build hook". See
# docs/astro-next/baseline/findings.md section 1. A removal is not a hook.
#
# What the two directives below add is not a relaxation of that rule but a way
# to state its exceptions ONE LINE AT A TIME, so that an UNDECLARED removal
# still fails exactly as it did before. The product rule they enforce is that
# no Chromium behaviour is removed unless the change is strictly a refactor
# extracting a generic extension point with behaviour preserved.
#
#   substitute <path> issue=<n> reason=<slug>
#       from  |<literal>
#       to    |<literal>
#       guard |<the #define that gives the macro its default>
#
#                 The stronger of the two, and the one to reach for first,
#                 because it BLESSES NOTHING. It accounts for a removed line
#                 only when replacing `from` with `to` in that line yields a
#                 line that is actually present among the ADDED lines — the
#                 removal is paired with its recomposed form, and a removal
#                 that merely deleted the literal matches nothing and is
#                 reported exactly as if no record existed. `guard` must appear
#                 among the added lines too, which is what separates "the
#                 literal moved behind a macro that still expands to it" from
#                 "the literal left the file and the macro comes from somewhere
#                 else". A paired addition does not count against max-added: a
#                 recomposed upstream line is the same line, not new surface.
#
#   remove <path> issue=<n> reason=<slug> |<the exact upstream line>
#
#                 The weaker one, for a removal no substitution can pair: an
#                 assertion or a literal branch REPLACED by a generic test.
#                 One record per removed line, matched on exact content, in a
#                 multiset — declaring a line once permits removing it once.
#                 There is no pattern form and no count form on purpose: both
#                 would let one declaration cover a family of similar lines,
#                 and "similar" is the judgement this file exists to take away
#                 from whoever is in a hurry.
#
# Everything after the first `|` is the upstream line byte for byte: no comment
# stripping, no trimming. Both directives are joined in BOTH directions — a
# declaration naming a line the integration no longer removes is stale and
# fails, and a substitution that pairs nothing fails, so neither can rot into a
# standing permission. Both require a LIVE entry for the same path, because a
# removal is only reviewable beside the entry that caps and owns the file's
# additions.
#
# Comments and blank lines are ignored, except inside a `remove` or
# `substitute` record where `#` is upstream content. Paths are relative to the
# Chromium source root and must not contain "..".

# --- #7: the minimum needed to build and reach //astro -----------------------

# chrome/browser/BUILD.gn — the build-graph edge.
#
# Cap arithmetic (minimum-chromium-hooks.md §A1): the designed shape is one
# `import()` of an Astro-owned .gni plus two list appends — `deps +=` and
# `allow_circular_includes_from +=` — which is 3 lines. The tree currently
# carries the pre-.gni variant: two `deps` labels plus one
# `allow_circular_includes_from` label, also 3. §A1 measures that BOTH deps
# labels are load-bearing (GN requires every allow_circular_includes_from entry
# to also appear in deps, so the concrete `//astro/browser` is mandatory; the
# roll-up `//astro:browser` is what causes astro/BUILD.gn to be parsed at all,
# and dropping it fails SILENTLY). The cap is 4 rather than 3 so the `import()`
# line can coexist with the concrete labels for the one revision that migrates
# between the two forms. It is not 9, which is what the file's raw diff holds.
gn-append     chrome/browser/BUILD.gn                       owner=oxy-browser issue=7  max-added=4

# chrome/browser/DEPS — the checkdeps include rule.
# Cap arithmetic (§A2): one `"+astro",` entry. Exactly one line, forever; a
# second line here would mean a second module, which is a different review.
include-rule  chrome/browser/DEPS                           owner=oxy-browser issue=7  max-added=1

# chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main.cc — the product-initialization entry point.
# Cap arithmetic (§A3, and its own delta table): one `#include` plus one
# `AddParts` call = 2. §C1 establishes that WebUI config registration rides on
# this same hook rather than costing a further Chromium-owned file, so this cap
# does not need to grow when #14 lands.
call-hook     chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main.cc         owner=oxy-browser issue=7  max-added=2

# --- #11: the astro:// scheme, chrome/-side ----------------------------------

# chrome/common/BUILD.gn — the build-graph edge for //astro/common.
# Cap arithmetic: one `public_deps` label. Same reasoning as chrome/browser's
# entry, minus the circular-include allowance, which `chrome/common` does not
# need because //astro/common does not depend back on it.
gn-append     chrome/common/BUILD.gn                        owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=1

# chrome/common/DEPS — the checkdeps include rule for the common layer.
# Cap arithmetic (§A2's reasoning, applied to the second layer): one line.
include-rule  chrome/common/DEPS                            owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=1

# chrome/common/chrome_content_client.cc — scheme registration.
# Cap arithmetic (§B, astro-scheme-hooks.md §2.1): one `#include` plus one call
# into astro::, the same shape as chrome_browser_main.cc = 2. The `Schemes*`
# out-parameter is upstream's own extension mechanism, so every per-scheme
# policy decision lives behind the call in //astro rather than here.
call-hook     chrome/common/chrome_content_client.cc        owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=2

# chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.h — the chrome/-side DECLARATION
# of the two embedder extension points content/ gained below.
#
# `embedder-hook` rather than `call-hook` because an override declaration names
# no Astro symbol and never can: it is upstream's own virtual, spelled as
# upstream spells it. There is nothing here for a call-hook's block rule to
# find, which is the case that shape's guidance means by "never grant it to a
# file that a call-hook would fit" — a call-hook does not fit.
#
# Cap arithmetic: 2 methods x 2 declaration lines = 4. 6 leaves room for a
# third trust level's declaration without ratifying an unreviewed comment
# block; the reasoning for WHY the two lists are separate lives on the content/
# declaration, not on this override.
embedder-hook chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.h owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=6

# chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc — the chrome/-side
# IMPLEMENTATION, which is where the answer comes from //astro.
#
# This one IS a call-hook: its whole body is three calls into astro::, and the
# lines that are not are the signature upstream's virtual dictates and the
# braces closing it.
#
# Cap arithmetic: 2 new overrides x (2 signature lines + 1 astro:: call + 1
# closing brace) = 8, plus the #include and the astro:: call added to the
# existing GetAdditionalWebUISchemes = 10. 12 gives that ~15% headroom.
call-hook     chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=12

# chrome/browser/profiles/profile_io_data.cc — IsHandledProtocol.
#
# Not routing, and the comment in the tree is the reason this is not a
# one-liner: the FALSE branch of this question is an escape hatch.
# ChildProcessSecurityPolicyImpl::CanRequestURL ends with `return
# !GetContentClient()->browser()->IsHandledURL(url)`, permitting a URL
# precisely BECAUSE an unhandled one is assumed destined for an external
# application. An unknown astro:// HOST must therefore still be a handled
# SCHEME, and fail inside the browser rather than reaching ShellExecute.
#
# Cap arithmetic: one #include, one `if (astro::IsAstroScheme(scheme))` and the
# two lines closing it = 4, plus a comment budget of 10 for the escape-hatch
# reasoning above = 14.
call-hook     chrome/browser/profiles/profile_io_data.cc     owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=14

# --- #11: the internal URL scheme composed from one macro ---------------------

# build/config/BUILDCONFIG.gn — the config that carries the scheme define to
# every target.
#
# Scoping it to one target does not work: ~2,057 call sites across chrome/
# include chrome/common/webui_url_constants.h, so a narrower define produces a
# binary where some constants say astro:// and others still say chrome://.
#
# Cap arithmetic: one config label. 9 covers it plus the comment recording why
# the define cannot be scoped narrowly, which is the kind of thing that gets
# "simplified" by the next person to read this file.
gn-append     build/config/BUILDCONFIG.gn                    owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=9

# chrome/common/webui_url_constants.h — 177 hand-spelled scheme literals
# replaced by composition from one macro.
#
# This is the largest entry in the file by raw diff and the smallest by
# reviewed surface, and the difference is the whole point of the `substitute`
# records below. 175 upstream lines are removed and 175 recomposed lines take
# their place; each pair is proved mechanically, so what remains against the
# cap is the macro block and its commentary — 33 lines.
#
# `embedder-hook`: the extension point IS the macro. An embedder overrides
# CHROME_UI_SCHEME_LITERAL and every constant follows; adjacent string literals
# concatenate at compile time, so the constants keep their names, their types
# and their default values, and none of the ~2,057 call sites changes.
#
# Cap arithmetic: 2 `#if !defined` guards x 3 lines = 6, 4 prefix `#define`s
# plus one line continuation = 5, and a comment budget of 25 for the
# measured-symptom record = 36. 40 gives that ~10% headroom.
embedder-hook chrome/common/webui_url_constants.h            owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=40

# The three substitutions that account for all 175 removals. A fourth —
# u"chrome-untrusted:// — was written and deleted again: the file has no
# UTF-16 untrusted constant, so the record paired nothing and the gate refused
# it as stale. That is the mechanism working, and it is why these are not
# written from the design.
#
# Behaviour preservation is what each `guard` states: with the default
# CHROME_UI_SCHEME_LITERAL the composed constants read exactly what they read
# before, verified in the browser — chrome://settings/ still loads.
substitute chrome/common/webui_url_constants.h issue=11 reason=scheme-literal-composed
    from  |"chrome://
    to    |CHROME_UI_URL_PREFIX "
    guard |#define CHROME_UI_URL_PREFIX CHROME_UI_SCHEME_LITERAL "://"
substitute chrome/common/webui_url_constants.h issue=11 reason=scheme-literal-composed
    from  |"chrome-untrusted://
    to    |CHROME_UI_UNTRUSTED_URL_PREFIX "
    guard |#define CHROME_UI_UNTRUSTED_URL_PREFIX CHROME_UI_UNTRUSTED_SCHEME_LITERAL "://"
substitute chrome/common/webui_url_constants.h issue=11 reason=scheme-literal-composed
    from  |u"chrome://
    to    |CHROME_UI_URL_PREFIX16 u"
    guard |#define CHROME_UI_URL_PREFIX16 u"" CHROME_UI_SCHEME_LITERAL u"://"

# --- #11/#12: the trusted/untrusted WebUI scheme split, content/-side --------
#
# These two are `embedder-hook`, the weakest shape, and the reason is stated
# rather than assumed: astro-scheme-hooks.md §2.3 measures that WebUIConfigMap
# hard-codes `kChromeUIScheme` in a `CHECK_EQ`, and minimum-chromium-hooks.md
# §C1 records that this single `CHECK_EQ` is the exact boundary of the "WebUI
# configs need no hook" claim. Generalising it cannot be done with a call into
# astro::, because content/ must stay embedder-agnostic — so these files gain
# upstream-shaped code that names no Astro symbol, and the cap plus the removal
# rule are the only things bounding them.

# content/public/browser/content_browser_client.h — two new virtual embedder
# extension points, GetAdditionalTrustedWebUISchemes and
# GetAdditionalUntrustedWebUISchemes.
#
# Cap arithmetic (astro-scheme-hooks.md §2.2 and §2.12): 2 methods x 2
# declaration lines = 4, plus the blank separating them, plus the doc comment
# that records WHY the two lists are separate — §2.12 establishes that trust is
# per-controller and per-scheme rather than a category, and a merged list would
# push that decision into every future call site. Comment budget 20, giving 25;
# 28 leaves room for a third trust level's declaration without ratifying the
# current 20-line content.
embedder-hook content/public/browser/content_browser_client.h owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=28

# content/public/browser/webui_config_map.cc — the scheme literals replaced by
# the embedder-supplied lists declared above.
#
# Cap arithmetic (astro-scheme-hooks.md §2.3): 5 includes + 2 memoised
# NoDestructor accessors at ~9 lines each (18) + a `Contains` helper (3) + the
# disjointness CHECK the two-list design requires (8) + an anonymous namespace
# (2) + 3 call-site replacements (7) + the doc comment carrying the security
# rationale (20) = 63. 72 gives that shape ~15% headroom.
#
embedder-hook content/public/browser/webui_config_map.cc     owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=72

# The 4 removals, and this file is the reason the `remove` directive exists.
# The gate found them before anything else did, and refused — correctly, because
# "these are replaced by something stronger" is a claim, and the 007 record is
# what a claim decided by whoever runs the build next costs.
#
# The claim, now stated where it can be checked: each of these four hard-codes
# the identity of a WebUI scheme, and each is replaced by membership in the
# trusted or untrusted set the embedder supplies. The check is not weakened —
# with the embedder supplying nothing, the sets hold exactly {chrome} and
# {chrome-untrusted} and the assertions fire on exactly the same inputs. What
# changes is that a second trusted scheme is now expressible, which is the whole
# of #11.
remove content/public/browser/webui_config_map.cc issue=11 reason=scheme-set-membership |  CHECK_EQ(config->scheme(), kChromeUIScheme);
remove content/public/browser/webui_config_map.cc issue=11 reason=scheme-set-membership |  CHECK_EQ(config->scheme(), kChromeUIUntrustedScheme);
remove content/public/browser/webui_config_map.cc issue=11 reason=scheme-set-membership |  if (url.GetScheme() != kChromeUIScheme &&
remove content/public/browser/webui_config_map.cc issue=11 reason=scheme-set-membership |      url.GetScheme() != kChromeUIUntrustedScheme) {

# --- #11: the content/-layer hooks that have now landed ----------------------
#
# These three were `planned` and are promoted here, which is what `planned` is
# for: the cap was reviewed in the calm and is being confirmed, not invented at
# the moment the hook landed. Where the arithmetic has changed, the new
# arithmetic is shown rather than the number quietly raised.

# §2.5 / §2.12 — the origin-keyed data-source lookup tested the untrusted
# scheme literally, and every other scheme fell through to a bare-HOST lookup.
# With more than one WebUI scheme that is not a shortcut but a security
# collapse: astro://test/ and astro-untrusted://test/ both reduce to the key
# "test", so one data source answers for two principals that exist precisely to
# be separate. Origin is now tried first, host second.
# Cap arithmetic, unchanged from the planned entry: the origin-keyed lookup
# rewritten across both scheme sets (~14) plus the comment recording the
# collapse (~12) = 26; 30 gives that ~15% headroom.
embedder-hook content/browser/webui/url_data_manager_backend.cc owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=30

# The 5 removals: the literal untrusted branch and the host-only lookup body it
# guarded, replaced by an origin-first lookup that falls back to host. The
# chrome:// sources that were host-registered still resolve by host, so no
# existing source changes behaviour; what changes is that an origin-registered
# source is no longer reachable under another scheme's key.
remove content/browser/webui/url_data_manager_backend.cc issue=11 reason=origin-before-host |  // chrome-untrusted:// sources keys are of the form "chrome-untrusted://host".
remove content/browser/webui/url_data_manager_backend.cc issue=11 reason=origin-before-host |  if (url.GetScheme() == kChromeUIUntrustedScheme) {
remove content/browser/webui/url_data_manager_backend.cc issue=11 reason=origin-before-host |    if (i == data_sources_.end())
remove content/browser/webui/url_data_manager_backend.cc issue=11 reason=origin-before-host |      return nullptr;
remove content/browser/webui/url_data_manager_backend.cc issue=11 reason=origin-before-host |    return i->second.get();

# §2.5 / §2.9 — WebUIDataSourceImpl::GetOrigin CHECKed that a source name which
# parses as a URL is chrome-untrusted://, so an astro-untrusted:// source name
# crashed. The origin now comes from the scheme the name actually carries.
# Cap arithmetic, unchanged: ~8 lines of logic plus the comment recording which
# list is authoritative. 20.
embedder-hook content/browser/webui/web_ui_data_source_impl.cc owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=20

# The 3 removals: the CHECK pinning the name form to one scheme, and the two
# comment lines asserting that form. The CHECK that the resulting origin is not
# opaque — which is what actually has to hold — is still there.
remove content/browser/webui/web_ui_data_source_impl.cc issue=11 reason=origin-from-source-name |    // |source_name_| must be of the form "chrome-untrusted://some-host/",
remove content/browser/webui/web_ui_data_source_impl.cc issue=11 reason=origin-from-source-name |    // which means it serves URLs of the form "chrome-untrusted://some-host/".
remove content/browser/webui/web_ui_data_source_impl.cc issue=11 reason=origin-from-source-name |    CHECK(url.SchemeIs(kChromeUIUntrustedScheme));

# §2.5 — WebUIImpl's requestable-scheme set was the literal pair
# {kChromeUIScheme, file}. That silently became "shared WebUI assets are
# reachable only from Chromium's own scheme" once an embedder could register
# another one, and the symptom is not a permission error anybody would
# recognise: the page navigates, commits and renders BLANK, because every
# chrome://resources/... import it makes is refused as cross-origin.
#
# Cap arithmetic, RAISED from the planned 12, which assumed one predicate
# replacement: the set is derived by a helper instead, because the value is
# needed in a member initialiser list where a predicate cannot go. Helper
# signature, 3 body lines and its brace = 5, the anonymous namespace around it
# = 2, the initialiser-list replacement = 1, so 8 code lines against the 1
# planned. Comment budget 24 for the blank-page symptom and for the sentence
# limiting this to SUBRESOURCE access — it says nothing about bindings — = 32.
# 34 gives that ~6% headroom.
embedder-hook content/browser/webui/web_ui_impl.cc            owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=34

# The 1 removal: the literal pair, replaced by the registered WebUI schemes
# plus file. With the embedder supplying nothing the set is {chrome, file},
# which is what it was.
remove content/browser/webui/web_ui_impl.cc issue=11 reason=registered-webui-schemes |    : requestable_schemes_({kChromeUIScheme, url::kFileScheme}),

# content/public/browser/url_data_source.cc — URLDataSource::ShouldServiceRequest
# tested a hardcoded three-scheme list. That was correct while chrome and
# chrome-untrusted were the only WebUI schemes a browser could have; with an
# embedder able to add one it became a rule that upstream data sources serve
# ONLY Chromium's schemes, and a WebUI reused under an embedder scheme
# registers fine, constructs fine, then fails at load with ERR_INVALID_URL.
#
# This does NOT widen trust: the list is the one used for LOADER ROUTING
# (URLDataManagerBackend::GetWebUISchemes, built from
# ContentBrowserClient::GetAdditionalWebUISchemes). Whether a scheme may hold
# privileged bindings is the separate question the two sets in WebUIConfigMap
# answer.
#
# Cap arithmetic: 4 includes + the devtools early return (3) + the membership
# test (3) = 10, plus a comment budget of 20 for the ERR_INVALID_URL symptom
# and the does-not-widen-trust paragraph = 30. 32 gives that ~7% headroom.
embedder-hook content/public/browser/url_data_source.cc      owner=oxy-browser issue=11 max-added=32

# The 2 removals: the hardcoded disjunction, now the devtools scheme tested
# separately plus membership in the registered WebUI schemes.
remove content/public/browser/url_data_source.cc issue=11 reason=registered-webui-schemes |  return url.SchemeIs(kChromeDevToolsScheme) || url.SchemeIs(kChromeUIScheme) ||
remove content/public/browser/url_data_source.cc issue=11 reason=registered-webui-schemes |         url.SchemeIs(kChromeUIUntrustedScheme);

# --- Planned: declared, capped, and granting nothing -------------------------
#
# The remaining content/-layer hook astro-scheme-hooks.md identifies. It is
# declared now so its cap is reviewed in the calm rather than invented at the
# moment it lands; it FAILS the gate the instant its file starts to change, and
# the fix is to promote the entry to a live shape by review.

# §2.6 — IsWebUIScheme / HasWebUIScheme / HasWebUIOrigin: a three-element
# literal with NO embedder hook at all, and 25+ call sites including the
# should-fork decision and IsSafeRedirectTarget. §2.6 calls this the sharpest
# blocker and names the one non-trivial design question in the whole document:
# the answer must be available in the RENDERER, where there is no
# ContentBrowserClient, so the list has to come from a common-layer store
# populated during RegisterContentSchemes rather than from the browser client.
# Cap arithmetic: a common-layer store (accessor + setter + storage, ~15),
# three predicates rewritten (~12), and the comment that has to explain why
# this one cannot use ContentBrowserClient (~13). 40.
planned       content/public/common/url_utils.cc              owner=oxy-browser issue=12 max-added=40
